// Baseline for the default headless scenario (issue #868, re-recorded #905).
//
// Measured by `--sim-seconds 60` against assets/worlds/default.toml, release
// build.
//
// RE-RECORDED ON THE MACHINE THAT COMPARES AGAINST IT. The first version of
// this file was written from three runs on a developer desktop and warned by
// +100% (sim.tick) and +114% (sim.run) on every CI run, because a
// GitHub-hosted runner is materially slower. Issue #905 replaced those numbers
// with the runner's own, adopted from the perf-capture artifact of CI run
// 30688920069 — the green main build of e87c8711, ubuntu-latest. The
// provenance block below records the same thing in the machine's words.
//
// Tolerances stay wider than vellum-perf's defaults (0.25 / 1.0), and now for
// the only reason left: the runner is shared, its neighbours move wall clock,
// and a tolerance that cries wolf gets ignored rather than obeyed. They are no
// longer compensating for the wrong machine.
//
// THIS SCENARIO DOES NOT GATE. Wall-clock on a shared runner fails the second
// half of the gating rule in src/perf/mod.rs — a red build here could be a
// neighbour. Reviewed again post-demo, against the observed spread of a run of
// green captures rather than against a hope.
//
// sim.tick is held at p95 rather than max on purpose. The first tick of a run
// loads assets and costs tens of milliseconds; that is startup, not
// steady-state simulation, and a max-based expectation would measure the
// loader. sim.run is a single sample per run, so its statistic is moot — mean
// is kept because that is what the file has always said.
//
// ── Recorded from a capture ─────────────────────────────────
// Written by `phoenix-perf adopt` from a headless-native capture of scenario "headless-default".
// build: release   device: Linux
// rev:   e87c8711ae290bba752d6f98e7229b62739c2b4f
//
// Edit the prose ABOVE this block; re-recording rewrites from here down, and
// carries every statistic and tolerance over unchanged. The numbers are the
// capture's; the tolerances are yours.
(
    scenario: "headless-default",
    expectations: {
        "sim.run": (
            unit: seconds,
            statistic: mean,
            expected: 5.144825412,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.5,
                fail: 2.0,
            ),
        ),
        "sim.tick": (
            unit: millis,
            statistic: p95,
            expected: 1.5613720000000002,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.5,
                fail: 2.0,
            ),
        ),
    },
)
